  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $f(x)=7{x}^{2}-2$ at the point $(2,26)$ intersects the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=7{x}^{2}-2.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{2}-2)=14x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(2)=14(2)=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(26) & =28(x-2) \\\\ y & =28x-56+26 \\\\ y & =28x-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =28(0)-30 \\\\ y & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $(0,-30).$",
